kubeos-dashboard (0.1.0-19) trixie; urgency=medium

  * FIX, and it broke a shipped page: clicking Helm showed the Helm releases page but the SIDEBAR put
    "Releases" and "Install" under Disks, and the Disks pages (LVM / LVM-Thin) disappeared. User, with a
    screenshot ("worst.png"): "when i click helm it going to disks". Cause: disks.tsx moved the Disks
    entry in the rendered sidebar with DOM surgery (Headlamp gives a plugin no ordering API, so the group
    was relocated to sit right after Storage by re-seating sibling nodes). That code decided which child
    list belonged to Storage BY EXCLUSION - "the list that is not ours" - which was only true while one
    plugin group could be expanded at a time. The new Helm group is a second expandable group, so the
    function took Helm's child list for Storage's and moved it after Disks: the Helm tree vanished into
    the Disks group and Disks' own children were left out of place. Worse, any DOM move invalidates
    React's list reconciliation, so React then re-inserted the next group's list at the wrong index -
    which is why a narrower rewrite of the same surgery still produced a wrong sidebar.
  * The surgery is GONE. The Disks group is now a plain top-level entry and its place comes from
    registration order (disks.tsx is imported first among the KubeOS pages, so it appears before Join
    Cluster / Helm / Snapshots / Rebalance, right after Headlamp's own entries). Cost, stated plainly:
    Disks is no longer immediately below Storage. A sidebar in a slightly different order is a much
    smaller problem than a page that cannot be reached, and no further plugin group can be broken by it.
  * Verified in a real browser in both directions: opening Helm shows its own child list [Releases,
    Install] directly under Helm and navigates to /kubeos-helm/releases; opening Disks shows [LVM,
    LVM-Thin] under Disks and navigates to /kubeos-disks/lvm; going back to Helm is still correct.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 14:10:00 +0530

kubeos-dashboard (0.1.0-18) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The read-only "Apps" page becomes a working "Helm" section. The user's words: "rename Apps to
    Helm and add important operations to dashboard like what user will do with helm command in
    terminal.. i just don't want to see only information". So it is now a sidebar GROUP with two
    children, "Releases" (the old page, plus buttons) and "Install" (a form), and the operations a
    terminal user runs are the ones it offers:
      - helm install    -> Install: pick an uploaded chart (or a chart path on the node), name,
                           namespace, values YAML, PREVIEW, then Install
      - helm upgrade    -> Releases > Upgrade: values prefilled from the release, chart optional
                           (the node reuses the uploaded chart it was installed from), PREVIEW,
                           then Upgrade
      - helm history    -> already there, and now the source of the rollback choices
      - helm rollback   -> Releases > Rollback: pick a revision, optional preview, typed confirm
      - helm uninstall  -> Releases > Uninstall: typed confirmation of the release name, and the
                           node refuses kubeos-system / kube-system no matter what the page asks
      - helm get values -> shown, and edited in YAML (the same text helm itself would print)
    Charts are uploaded from the browser (`POST /helm/upload`, 64 MiB cap, the stored filename is
    generated by the node, and the archive must really contain a Chart.yaml before it is offered
    to helm); the last 20 uploads are kept.
  * STILL NO POD, JOB OR EXEC (user decision 2026-11-12): every operation is one `helm` call on
    the host through kubeos-console.service, with an argument list, one at a time (a second
    mutation is refused while one runs - measured with four simultaneous installs: three refused,
    one applied), each logged with the client address, and every release name, namespace, revision
    and chart path validated before it can reach argv.
  * PREVIEW IS ALWAYS `--dry-run=client`, never `server`: client mode renders the chart without
    contacting the API, so a preview cannot create a namespace even where helm's own
    `--create-namespace --dry-run=server` combination has been reported to persist one. The apply
    button stays disabled until the form has been previewed and has not been edited since.
  * Two defects found while testing it, both fixed here:
      - a %-format mistake in the success log made the node answer a *completed* install/upgrade
        with a dropped connection, which reads as "it did not happen" and invites a retry of
        something already applied. The handler now answers 500 with "the node ran the Helm command
        but could not report it" instead of dying, and the format string is correct.
      - MUI closes a Dialog on a backdrop click, so a click just outside the paper threw away a
        half-filled form; and because the page and the dialog both have a button called "Upgrade",
        the closed dialog silently turned the next click into a no-op. Backdrop clicks no longer
        close a Helm dialog (Escape still does).
  * Verified in a real browser (headless Chromium logging in, clicking the sidebar): the group
    expands to Releases/Install, Install disabled the apply button until preview and rendered the
    ConfigMap in the preview, installed webdemo/webdemo, upgraded it through the dialog, refused
    the uninstall button until the release name was typed, then uninstalled it. No Pod or Job was
    created at any point.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 13:40:00 +0530

kubeos-dashboard (0.1.0-17) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Two defects the first screenshot of the working page showed, both mine:
    - "Objects" read `—` for every release. helm v4 returns `info.resources` as a DICT keyed by
      `<apiVersion>/<kind>` whose values are the objects of that kind
      (`{"v1/ConfigMap": [ {...} ]}`), not the flat list v3 had, so `Array.isArray` was false and
      the page fell back to a dash. The count now sums the per-kind lists and still accepts a plain
      array. My earlier check said "resources: 1" because `len()` of a one-key dict is also 1 - an
      agreeing number that proved nothing.
    - The title was printed twice ("Apps / Apps / Helm releases on this node …") because the page
      drew its own heading inside a section box that already has one. The subtitle and the Refresh
      button remain.
  * Same read-only page otherwise; verified by logging into the dashboard in a headless browser,
    clicking "Apps" in the sidebar and reading the rendered table and detail panel back from the
    DOM, with a two-ConfigMap demo chart so the object count is not another accidental 1.

