kubeos-dashboard (0.1.0-15) trixie; urgency=medium

  * New "Apps" page: what Helm has deployed on this node, from the dashboard instead of an SSH
    session (user asked for Helm in the dashboard, 2026-11-22). It lists every release across
    namespaces (name, namespace, chart, app version, revision, status, updated) and, for the
    selected one, its status and chart notes, the values the user supplied, the revision history
    and the rendered manifest; it also shows which chart repositories are configured.
    READ-ONLY on purpose: a page that can only look cannot break a cluster, so it ships first.
    Install, upgrade, rollback and uninstall are the next change, each with a dry-run diff and a
    typed confirmation, and with uninstall refused for kubeos-system and kube-system (user
    decision 2026-11-22).
  * It is NOT a Pod, a Job or an exec (user decision 2026-11-12): the page calls
    kubeos-console.service, which runs `helm` on the host with an argument list - the same gate
    as the LVM actions (token + Origin, one action at a time, 60 s timeout, logged with the client
    address). Release names and namespaces are validated against a DNS-label pattern before they
    reach argv, so nothing user-supplied can become an option or a shell word.
  * Measured on helm v4.3.0 (not assumed, and it changed the code): `list -A -o json`,
    `history -o json` and `get values -o json` behave as expected, but **`status -o json` no
    longer carries a `chart` block** (v3 did) - it has apply_method/info/manifest/name/namespace/
    version, with the objects it created under info.resources. The page therefore takes the chart
    and app version from the list row and shows the resource count from info.resources.
  * Two pre-existing type errors fixed while in here, both in code this page depends on:
    `certificateHint(config.port)` passed an argument to a function that takes none, and the
    Shell button's socket error path interpolated `${hostname}` - a name that does not exist -
    so it threw a ReferenceError exactly when the shell had already failed and the hint never
    appeared. `npm run tsc` is clean now; the message is the one-origin one that 0.1.0-13 made true.
  * Verified on 192.168.122.5 with a throwaway ConfigMap-only chart: both endpoints answered,
    an injection-shaped release name got 400, a missing release got 404, a bad token 403, no Pod
    or Job was created by any of it, and the test release was removed afterwards.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 12:05:00 +0530

kubeos-dashboard (0.1.0-14) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The dashboard is drawn at 70%, the size it was signed off at (user, 2026-11-22:
    sizedashboard.png is the product read in a 1366x768 window at 70% browser zoom). Headlamp lays
    itself out for a wide viewport, so at 100% in that window the sidebar, the four Overview cards
    and the Events table are cramped and the whole UI reads as too big.
    The gateway now injects html{zoom:0.7} into <head> of every proxied Headlamp page, before the
    first paint. `zoom` is the only lever that reproduces browser zoom: it scales the layout AND
    enlarges the CSS viewport (~1366 px becomes ~1950 px), so MUI's breakpoints pick the wide
    layout. Smaller typography/density would leave the layout believing it has 1366 px, and
    transform: scale() would shrink the 1366 px render into a corner with a 30% gap.
  * The scaled shell now fills the window. Headlamp's outermost element is
    Box{sx:{display:"flex",height:"100dvh"}} - a viewport unit, which `zoom` does not rescale - so
    it was laid out at the window's real height and then drawn at 70%, leaving an empty band under
    the UI (user: notstreched.png). The shell's height is divided by the factor to compensate.
    First attempt applied the height to html/body/#root, one level above the sized element, and
    could not have worked; the rule now targets #root>div as well.
  * Proxied HTML is served Cache-Control: no-store. It carries the injected rules, so a corrected
    rule looked like "nothing changed" while the browser re-used the cached shell.
  * KUBEOS_UI_ZOOM overrides the factor per node (1 = unscaled), and any browser can override it
    for itself once with ?zoom=0.68 (kept in localStorage), which moves the divided heights too.
    Browsers without `zoom` (Firefox before 126) ignore the rule and show 100%.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Sun, 22 Nov 2026 11:25:00 +0530

kubeos-dashboard (0.1.0-13) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The console hop now presents the console's own Origin. console.py accepts a handshake or an
    action only when the hostname in Origin equals the hostname in Host, and 0.1.0-12 forwarded
    the browser's origin (https://<node>:30443) while sending Host: 127.0.0.1:30444 - so every
    Shell click through the gateway was answered 403 "bad origin" and the page said "the host
    console closed this session" (measured in Firefox 2026-11-21). The origin the console expects
    is now set on this internal hop; its check is unchanged for anything talking to it directly.
    Why 0.1.0-12's own test passed: the test client sent no Origin header at all, which is why a
    real browser was needed to find this.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Sat, 21 Nov 2026 23:25:00 +0530

kubeos-dashboard (0.1.0-12) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The browser no longer talks to the node console's own port. A browser treats every port as a
    separate site, so reaching the console on :30444 meant a SECOND certificate exception, and
    until somebody accepted it the Shell button failed with "could not reach the node console on
    port 30444" - a step no user can be expected to know about. The gateway that already serves
    :30443 now proxies /console-api/* to the console internally, so the whole product has one
    origin and one certificate.
  * Configured, not hardcoded: KUBEOS_CONSOLE_URL (default https://127.0.0.1:30444),
    KUBEOS_CONSOLE_CA (default /etc/kubeos-dashboard/ca.crt) and KUBEOS_CONSOLE_TLS_VERIFY
    (default 1). The internal hop still VERIFIES the console's certificate against the cluster CA
    - only the name check is relaxed, because the hop dials 127.0.0.1 while the leaf carries the
    node's address. Verification is not switched off anywhere else.
  * The terminal WebSocket, restart/power-off and the snapshot download all use the same origin;
    the console token travels either as an Authorization header or as ?token= (a browser cannot
    set headers on a WebSocket) and is passed through untouched - access logs keep the path and
    drop the query, so a token never reaches the journal.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Sat, 21 Nov 2026 23:05:00 +0530

